// cutPointsItems: KP1 cutting at proved equivalences, KP2 false negatives, KP3 resolving them.
var cutPointsItems = []edakit.Item{
	{KP: 1, Q: "An equivalence checker has proved internal nodes u (design A) and u' (design B) equivalent. What does inserting a cut point there do?",
		A: "Replaces both nodes by one shared free variable in the later checks", D: []string{"Deletes the logic driving the outputs", "Adds a register at u", "Forces u and u' to 0"},
		Why: "Logic beyond the cut sees the same free input on both sides, so each later SAT call only reasons about the logic between cuts and outputs."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "Why do cut points make each SAT call smaller?",
		A: "The solver no longer reasons about the logic in front of the cut", D: []string{"They remove all XOR gates", "They shrink the input count of the design", "They make the miter unsatisfiable"},
		Why: "With the fan-in cone hidden behind a free variable, the remaining problem is a narrow slice of the circuit."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "A proof using cut points says the outputs are equivalent. Is that conclusion sound?",
		A: "Yes: a free variable allows every value the original logic could produce, and more", D: []string{"No: cut points can hide real differences", "Only if the cut nodes are registers", "Only for single-output designs"},
		Why: "Cutting over-approximates the behavior at the cut, so if the outputs agree for every value of the free variable they agree for the real ones."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "With cut points inserted, the output miter becomes satisfiable, but the designs are actually equivalent. What happened?",
		A: "The counterexample uses a combination of cut values that the real logic in front of the cuts can never produce", D: []string{"The cut points were not equivalent", "The SAT solver made an error", "The simulation signatures were wrong"},
		Why: "Two cut variables are treated as independent, but in the circuit they may be correlated; the solver exploits the impossible combination."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "Cut variables u and v drive a downstream gate. In the real circuit u = ¬v always. Why can this produce a false negative?",
		A: "The cut forgets the correlation, so the solver may set u = v, a state the circuit cannot reach", D: []string{"Inverters cannot be cut", "u and v must be cut at the same time", "Correlated signals make SAT unsound"},
		Why: "Free variables have no relationship to each other unless the checker keeps one."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "What is a false negative in equivalence checking?",
		A: "A reported difference that does not occur in the real designs", D: []string{"A missed difference between the designs", "A proof that takes too long", "A difference in a don't-care output"},
		Why: "False negatives are spurious counterexamples from over-approximation; a missed difference would be a soundness bug."},
	{KP: 2, Q: "Which situation makes false negatives from cut points most likely?",
		A: "Cutting at nodes whose values are strongly correlated, such as a signal and its re-encoded copy", D: []string{"Cutting at primary inputs", "Cutting at nodes with independent fan-in cones", "Cutting at the outputs"},
		Why: "If the cut signals are functionally independent, every combination is realizable and no spurious behavior appears."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "The checker gets a counterexample under cut points. What is its first step to decide whether it is real?",
		A: "Simulate the counterexample's primary-input values through the full logic without cuts", D: []string{"Accept it as a design bug", "Add more cut points", "Re-run the same SAT query"},
		Why: "If the full designs disagree on those inputs the bug is real; if not, the counterexample relied on impossible cut values."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "A counterexample is shown to be spurious. How does the checker refine the problem?",
		A: "Remove the offending cut (or move it toward the inputs) so the correlated logic is visible again", D: []string{"Insert the same cut again", "Declare non-equivalence", "Increase the number of random simulation patterns only"},
		Why: "Exposing the logic behind the cut restores the correlation, at the cost of a larger SAT problem; this is the same refine step as in CEGAR."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "Why do equivalence checkers not simply avoid cut points altogether?",
		A: "Without cuts, each SAT query sees the whole cone and can become too hard", D: []string{"Cuts are required for soundness", "SAT solvers cannot handle reconvergent fan-out", "Cuts are needed to match registers"},
		Why: "Cut points trade a small risk of false negatives for much smaller proofs; refinement handles the rare spurious case."},
	{KP: 3, Q: "Which picture describes the cut-point refinement loop?",
		A: "Over-approximate, check, test the counterexample on the concrete logic, and refine only if it is spurious", D: []string{"Under-approximate, check, and stop at the first counterexample", "Simulate until all nodes are proved", "Prove each node by BDDs and never refine"},
		Why: "It is abstraction refinement applied to combinational logic, which is why it shares its soundness argument with CEGAR."},
	{KP: 1, Q: "Where does a SAT-sweeping engine normally place cut points?",
		A: "At internal nodes it has just proved equivalent between the two designs", D: []string{"At randomly chosen gates", "At every register boundary only", "At nodes with different simulation signatures"},
		Why: "Proved-equivalent pairs are the natural cut points, because merging them loses no information about the designs' agreement."},
}

